I have a validated, runnable recipe. Here's what grounds it:

- **Discover/route:** The goal is per-speech-segment quality scoring on long recordings. From `catalog-tree` the relevant categories are `segment` (VAD) + `quality` (a scorer), plus `ingest`/`preprocess`/`export` for I/O. No filtering intent → I did **not** pull in filter/yield logic.
- **Blueprint match:** `context` returned an exact blueprint, `readspeech-quality-filter`, whose `topology_selection` maps **"per-speech-segment scores" → `Mono → VAD(fanout) → Filters → TimestampMapper`**, and the `vad-changes-granularity` pattern confirms VAD is how you go from one-number-per-file to one-score-per-speech-chunk. I adapted that shape.
- **Stage choice:** For "a quality score" I use `UTMOSFilterStage` — it emits a single overall MOS (`utmos_mos`, 0–5), the canonical one-number-per-clip quality score. `VADSegmentationStage` (Silero, bundled/CPU-friendly, its card lists "per-segment quality scoring" as a good_for) over `WhisperXVADStage` (heavier, downloads a model) — low-impact auto-pick.
- **Resolve:** none needed — this is a **scoring/annotation** request, not filtering, so there is no threshold to resolve. I set `action: annotate` + `mos_threshold: null` so every segment is kept and only the score is written (never silently dropping speech).
- **Validate:** `runnable: true`. The only iteration was the sink: routing the resident waveform straight into a JSONL writer triggered `tensor_into_sink`, so the pipeline ends at `AudioToDocumentStage`, the sanctioned sanitizing sink (strips tensors/audio blobs). `TimestampMapperStage` adds `original_file` + `start_ms`/`end_ms`/`segment_num` so each score maps back to a position in the long recording.

```recipe
stages:
  - {ref: ManifestReader, params: {manifest_path: REQUIRED}}
  - {ref: MonoConversionStage, params: {output_sample_rate: 16000, strict_sample_rate: false}}
  - {ref: VADSegmentationStage, params: {nested: false, min_duration_sec: 1.0, max_duration_sec: 30.0}}
  - {ref: UTMOSFilterStage, params: {action: annotate, mos_threshold: null, mode: auto}}
  - {ref: TimestampMapperStage, params: {}}
  - {ref: AudioToDocumentStage, params: {}}
```

This uses `VADSegmentationStage` (Silero VAD) to fan each long recording out into one row per detected speech chunk, then `UTMOSFilterStage` in `annotate` mode to write a per-segment overall MOS quality score (`utmos_mos`) — giving you one quality number per bit of speech instead of one per file, with `TimestampMapperStage` tagging each score with its source file and start/end so you can trace it back. Assumptions worth confirming: I normalize to mono/16 kHz up front (`MonoConversionStage`) since long recordings are usually stereo/varied-rate — drop it if your audio is already mono 16 kHz; I picked UTMOS's single overall MOS as "a quality score" (if you instead want a fine-grained breakdown — noise, reverb, coloration, etc. — swap in `SIGMOSFilterStage`, also in `annotate` mode); and note UTMOS is a *predictor* of human MOS (naturalness/overall quality), not a background-noise meter. Next step per the workflow would be a bounded `smoke` on ~10 files (which also needs your `manifest_path`) before any confirmed full run.
